{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790983743,"nonce":"EdLH2lKl/Hcb/8tY1NTCGCIg8cs=","title":"cb_window: re-export xwindow's host hook and choose an Android activity","message":"xwindow's native backend gains a hook a host calls before the first window opens, in the xwindow revision that closes xwindow's Android and iOS issues (3371df8a2a671b897b07b3687d5126be7b50273d392baa994c54c7dbaf98d8be, dfb8c96d8d00b824376afcf8b1b68afb8828378aca1266c931959db3eb981ed5). It is an adapter contract, described in xwindow's CONTRIBUTING.md under \"The host's hook\":\n\n    attach(events: EventLoop\u003c()\u003e, drive: Drive) -\u003e Result\u003c(), String\u003e\n    Drive::Pump            // the program pumps the host's loop in open/poll/wait\n    Drive::Turns(\u0026mut turn) // the host runs the loop; turn() is the program's turn\n\n- Android: the host builds the loop with the AndroidApp its android_main receives (EventLoop::builder().with_android_app(app)); either drive works.\n- iOS: winit cannot pump, so the host calls attach with Drive::Turns from main, and it never returns. A window opens only in a turn; poll and wait do not block, and the last empty one says when the next turn comes.\n\nWhen cb_window moves to that xwindow revision it needs to:\n\n1. Re-export the hook natively: `pub use backend::{attach, Drive};` and `pub use winit;`, so Caribou's mobile host builds the loop from the same winit.\n2. Enable one winit activity feature on Android. They cannot both be on (android-activity refuses to compile). hlwindow's shape: a default feature `android-native-activity = [\"winit/android-native-activity\"]` and an opt-in `android-game-activity = [\"winit/android-game-activity\"]`. Without one, a check for aarch64-linux-android fails in android-activity.\n3. Give Caribou's Android and iOS hosts the attach call: on Android in android_main before the program runs; on iOS as the main loop, with the program run in turns.\n\nThe same revision adds window operations to the declaration (icon, theme override, resize increments, enabled buttons, exclusive fullscreen with monitors' video modes, drag and drag-resize, the window menu, cursor hittest, IME purpose, inner position), which cb_window gets from the generated model without code of its own.\n\nVerified in xwindow with its stand-in adapter on macOS (both drives), the iOS simulator (turns) and the Android emulator (both drives).","files":null}]}